# Linux import check for decoded counts and real logarithms

This probe asks whether the exact decoded-count modules and the pinned real logarithm library can share one Lean environment on the observed Ubuntu runner. The original seven decoded-count theorems concern positive rational values. To form the new expression the observer takes the rational value at one table, table part, component and rule, casts it to a real number and applies the natural logarithm. It defines no theorem.

## Run the fixed inputs

The workflow accepts a push to `review/decoded-real-log-import-20261127` or a manual dispatch on that exact ref after the workflow exists on the default branch. The human release operator selects the repository and commits. Checkout has read-only content access and leaves its credentials outside repository configuration. The acquisition and Lean processes receive a fresh home, a fixed executable search path and no GitHub credentials.

The job uses the official Lean 4.34.1 Linux archive and verifies its complete digest, compiler revision and the two observed Linux executables. It obtains Mathlib at the named revision, reads the original package manifest, invokes its cache tool for `Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic`, verifies the eight dependency checkouts and compares 9,715 files for 1,943 imported modules with the earlier native Linux artifact. Full identities are in `origins.json` and `basic-cache-identities.json.gz`. The older library job read a larger import with some of the same dependencies. The two experiments share the toolchain, package cache and original mathematical sources.

The 30-member source archive contains 25 ordered modules, three original audit/control files and two original native output streams. It preserves each source byte. The stock lane builds the original 25 modules with the compiler, checks seven existing numerical and grammar assertions, compares the full types and axiom lists of seven existing theorems, freshly replays the audit module, and requires the original refusal of 16/9 in place of 9/16. The real-only lane reads nine named declarations, observes their transitive axiom rows, performs a fresh replay and requires the compiler to reject log(1) = 1 when given log(1) = 0. The combined lane checks the concrete real expression and five named declarations, replays their combined imports, and verifies that Lean rejects the real expression at type Nat. The real-only search path excludes the stock objects. Complete streams and status are separate for every lane.

The official fresh checker replays eligible definitions and theorems in a new environment with trust level zero. Its stock implementation skips unsafe or partial declarations and adds allowed axiom declarations. The seven original expression/dependency reports used a loaded environment at trust level 1025. The fresh-check and the expression reports measure different aspects of trust. This run does not produce a new expression report. A proof connecting the logarithm to an independently defined empirical average and a proof of executable or floating-point correspondence remain open.

## Resource and outcome evidence

One Linux CPU is available to the process and its descendants, and Lean uses one worker. Each native compile or fresh replay is limited to 600 seconds; source or cache setup has up to 1,200 seconds per long command. The whole managed run has 75 minutes inside a 90-minute GitHub job. The monitor samples the process group about every 100 ms, checks a 4 GiB aggregate resident-memory threshold, limits growth of used space on the scratch volume to 18 GiB, keeps 1 GiB free, and checks a 16 MiB threshold per native output stream. Resident-memory and disk measurements are sampled, not cgroup reservations. A quota, unavailable dependency, incomplete output or refused acquisition records that outcome. The recorded Darwin attempt could not load the smaller module because its compiled object was absent. The prior bare stock import succeeded on Darwin within the original 1 GiB guard. Neither event measures the fresh combined Linux run.

The workflow uploads `START`, `host.txt`, `RESULT.json`, `COMMANDS.json`, captured stdout/stderr and any exception reports. Each precondition, command outcome and blocked dependency remains identifiable. An interrupted runner can prevent artifact upload; the GitHub event and its terminal log then remain the evidence. Obtain the archive from the actual job and compare its run ID, attempt, commit and tree before reporting an import result.

For a local input check run `python3 -I -S -B audit/formal/decoded-real-log-import/self_test.py` and repeat with `-O`. These checks exercise the reader on fixed positive and deliberately altered records. The workflow invokes the separate native commands that produce the real Linux evidence. For a manual Linux replay, use an empty directory and pass its absolute path to `python3 -I -S -B audit/formal/decoded-real-log-import/probe.py --root DIR`.
